<<<
are, is. When one of these is required between a subject & a complement that differ in number (these things . . . a@ scandal), the verb must follow the number of the subject (are, not ts, a scandal).
===
**are, is.** When one of these is required between a subject & a complement that differ in number (*these things . . . a scandal*), the verb must follow the number of the subject (*are*, not *is*, a scandal).
>>>

<<<
The wages of sin is death is an archaism; we do not now say his wages is, but are, a pound a week; & we do not say a pound are, but is, his wages; when, as here, subject & complement can change places without alteration of sense,
===
*The wages of sin is death* is an archaism; we do not now say *his wages is*, but *are, a pound a week*; & we do not say *a pound are*, but *is, his wages*; when, as here, subject & complement can change places without alteration of sense,
>>>

